Argus-Core migration ownership and bootstrap
===========================================

Graph:

  base
    -> 3f1a9c2d7e60  historical core_sources table and nonunique hash index
    -> 8d3f39d3a946  unique partial content_hash index
    -> 8bd61bcfd002  applications and API keys
    -> dea3e0498bf6  audit logs (unchanged head)

The original root assumed that core_sources already existed. The new predecessor
reconstructs that historical schema, including ix_core_sources_content_hash;
the existing index revision then replaces it. No existing revision ID or upgrade/
downgrade body was changed. Only the original root's parent metadata changed.
Columns use the historical definitions; Python-side ORM defaults are not silently
converted to PostgreSQL server defaults. No deduplication or data cleanup occurs.

Compatibility and release precautions
-------------------------------------

An existing database recorded at dea3e0498bf6 stays at head. Alembic does not replay
the new ancestor; an upgrade to head performs no schema/data operations there.
The same holds for the ancestor portion of databases recorded at the existing
root/application revision; only their subsequent migrations run. Tests reconstruct
the original graph independently and verify all three existing revisions.

Changing ancestry is a deliberate historical-metadata repair, not a new release
head. Deploy the complete repaired script set together. Older checkouts cannot
resolve the new baseline ID, so do not mix migration runners from different script
sets. Obtain release approval if published migration metadata is immutable under
your deployment policy. Rehearse on an isolated backup restore before deployment.

An unversioned populated database is NOT automatically adopted. Do not blindly stamp
it, rerun the original root, or add IF NOT EXISTS to conceal incompatible objects.
Inventory and separately review adoption for such databases. The inspected local
studio-ai database was already at head and matched the models; this repair requires
no stamping, table recreation, or data change there. It was not used by these tests.

Downgrade semantics change at the new boundary: downgrading all the way to base now
drops core_sources as well as the other Core tables. That is destructive; the
automated round trip is ONLY for disposable databases. Never use downgrade-to-base
as a repair procedure on a populated database.

Target selection and ownership
------------------------------

alembic.ini resolves backend imports relative to the repository, independent of
the caller's working directory. Install the pinned Alembic requirement first.
An explicit sqlalchemy.url configuration takes precedence over application
settings. Otherwise DATABASE_URL/backend/.env selection behaves as before.
Programmatic callers can supply config.attributes['connection']; this uses only
that connection and does not open another database connection. Do not place secrets
in tracked configuration files. Verify the selected target before any migration.

CoreBase alone supplies target metadata. Global Chess tables (topics, analyses,
research sources and story clusters) remain excluded from autogeneration. The
filter is not an authorization boundary for arbitrary handwritten SQL; review new
revision operations for Core-only ownership.

Core startup now validates connectivity, revision and table presence read-only;
it never creates schemas or runs migrations. See backend/DATABASE_STARTUP.md for
the provision -> migrate -> start deployment sequence. Global Chess retains its
separate initialization. Migration/bootstrap/readiness tests explicitly forbid
create_all() and do not use the application integration fixture.

Automated verification
----------------------

Run backend/tests/integration/test_migration_bootstrap.py before application tests.
Set ARGUS_CORE_TEST_DATABASE_URL to the dedicated disposable argus_core_test
PostgreSQL control database and DATABASE_URL to an isolated target or sqlite://
for test collection. Never point either variable at development/production.

The migration fixture verifies current_database(), requires CREATEDB, and provisions
a new empty argus_core_migration_test_<uuid> database for each scenario. It checks
the new database identity and removes only the database it created. There is no
fallback to studio-ai or to the create_all()-based integration fixture. Cleanup
does not force-disconnect other clients; connection leaks fail visibly.

Coverage includes empty installation through the entire Alembic chain, the old
nonunique baseline index, metadata equivalence, original-root/application/head
compatibility, populated Core/Global Chess data preservation, no writes/DDL on an
existing head, ownership-aware autogeneration checks, isolated downgrade ownership,
duplicate rejection without data mutation, explicit URL selection, and CLI startup
from the repository root. Existing-head fixtures use temporary copies of the old
graph with its original parent pointer, not the repaired bootstrap.
